# Upgrade Your Dependency Management with pip-tools

Dependency management is one of the most important issues for every Software Engineer. The tool we choose for dependency management is fundamental and has a significant impact on many aspects of our applications. I want to talk about dependency management in Python, which plays a key role in the reproducibility of environments, sharing code, and deployment of our applications.

There are several Python package managers available. Some of the main ones include:

- [pip](https://pip.pypa.io/en/stable/)
- [Conda](https://conda.org/\)
- [poetry](https://python-poetry.org/)
- [pipenv](https://pipenv.pypa.io/en/latest/)
- [pdm](https://pdm-project.org/latest/usage/venv/)
- [rye](https://rye-up.com/)

It's worth exploring their features and benefits in the future (perhaps in my blog :). But, in this post, I would like to share my experience of using [pip-tools] (https://pypi.org/project/pip-tools/2.0.0/).

I've noticed that many Software Engineers use `pip`, using `requirements.txt` generated by `pip freeze`. But, this approach has a lot of disadvantages.

- `pip freeze` captures every package installed in the environment, including unnecessary. This can lead to cluttered `requirements.txt` files;
- `pip freeze` doesn't allow keep fresh version in our project requirements.txt won't reflect that, potentially leading to outdated dependencies;
- `pip freeze` doesn't capture any version constraints we might have specified when installing packages. But, these constraints help ensure compatibility between different versions of the same package.

In my study project, I tried `pip-tools` and realized the numerous advantages it offers. This is the reason I want to share my experience in this article.

So, what are the peculiarities of using `pip-tools`?

`pip-tools` is a set of utilities, which provide, keeping our packages fresh even when we've pinned them earlier.

I would like to describe short algorithm for usage of this tool just from official sources. It's very important — don't forget to activate [the virtual environments](https://packaging.python.org/en/latest/tutorials/installing-packages/#creating-virtual-environments\) 🙂

Then we should do several steps:

1. Install `python -m pip install pip-tools`;
2. In the root directory, we should create a file named `requirements.in`, for example:

```python
django
requests
pillow
```

3. Open terminal and run the command `pip-compile requirements.in` this will generate a new file `requirements.txt`
4. It's very important to take into account that `pip-tools = pip-compile + pip-sync`, so command `pip-sync requirements.txt` will synchronize the environment with the updated dependencies.

One of the main benefits of implementing this tool is the ability to separate development and production dependencies (optional). For example, in my Django project I have:

```
requirements/
├── dev.in
├── dev.txt
├── base.in
└── base.txt
```

In `base.in`:
```python
Django ~= 3.0
```

`base.txt:`

```python

This file is autogenerated by pip-compile with Python 3.11
by the following command:
pip-compile requirements/base.in
asgiref==3.7.2
# via django
django==3.2.21
# via -r base.in
pytz==2023.3.post1
# via django
sqlparse==0.4.4
# via django
```

`dev.in:`

```python
-r base.in
coverage
pylint
```

But, it's good practice to define several categories of our dependencies. You can even create separate for development, lint, tests and others. You can  take a look this example for for inspiration: [https://github.com/pypi/warehouse/tree/main/requirements\]\(https://github.com/pypi/warehouse/tree/main/requirements\)

There is `lock file` (in my case `dev.txt` and `base.txt`). `Lock file` ensures reproducibility of the project environment. By using the `pip-sync` command, all dependencies will be installed with the exact versions specified in the `dev.txt` and `base.txt`.

If we need to update dependencies, we can change their versions in the `base.in` file and then regenerate the `base.txt` file using the `pip-compile base.in` command. This will update the versions of the packages and their transitive dependencies according to the new requirements.

Constraints files are a way to specify additional version restrictions for packages, particularly transitive dependencies, in our Python projects. They work in conjunction with the primary requirements file (usually named `requirements.in` or another with extension `.in`). It helps in resolving conflicts that may arise when different parts of a project have conflicting dependencies. By pinning specific versions of transitive dependencies constraints files ensure compatibility between different packages and maintain a stable project environment.

Using `pip-tools`, we can automatically manage transitive dependencies. When we run the `pip-compile` command, `pip-tools` analyzes the requirements file and starts the dependency resolution process. `pip-tools` recursively identifies its own dependencies (transitive dependencies) and builds a complete dependency tree.

Why I recommend switching to `pip-tools`:

- **Dependency separation.** `pip-tools` allows separating our project's direct dependencies from the transitive ones. We can create distinct files for production, dev, tests, and others. This makes the dependency management more transparent and easier to understand.
- **Automatic management.** `pip-tools` automatically identifies and includes transitive dependencies in the generated `.txt` files. This ensures that all required packages are installed with compatible versions.
- **Streamlined updates.** `pip-tools` simplifies the process of updating dependencies. We can modify the versions of packages in the `requirements.in` file and regenerate the `requirements.txt` file using the `pip-compile` command. This updates the versions of the packages and their transitive dependencies according to the new.
- **Synchronization with `pip-sync`.** After generating the updated `requirements.txt` file, we can use the `pip-sync` command to synchronize our project environment with the specified dependencies. `pip-sync` ensures that only the packages listed in the `requirements.txt` file are installed, removing any extraneous packages. This helps maintain a clean and consistent environment.
- **Integration with other tools** (it was skipped in this post, complicated topic which I can discuss in another post :). I noticed in GitHub projects usage `tox` for managing dependencies. It's a good choice, but come back to this a little later.

So, using `pip-tools` we can significantly improve our dependency management workflow. Let's try this and share your experience.

Happy coding!


